Q: How to create a type-deducing tuple with neither copyable nor movable members? The tuple itself is a class member A: <p>You might define the prototype of <code>MakeContainerOfS</code> as follows:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...Ts&gt;
auto MakeContainerOfS(std::tuple&lt;Ts...&gt;) -&gt; std::tuple&lt;S&lt;Ts&gt;...&gt;;
</code></pre>
<p>You can see that the function doesn't need to have a body, we are just interested in the result type. We just provide as arguments the types of the input tuple to the <code>S</code> template class. In other words, we just work with types and not objects here, which is required since <code>S</code> objects can't be copied.</p>
<p>Then you can define <code>Container</code> like this:</p>
<pre><code>struct Container {

    static constexpr std::tuple t = std::make_tuple (1, 2.0f);

    decltype(MakeContainerOfS(t)) container = t;

    void Call() {
        std::apply([](auto &amp;...members) { (members.InterfaceMethod(), ...); }, container);
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>where you define a tuple <code>t</code> holding the values of interest. Note that we don't have to use explicitely <code>std::tuple&lt;int,float&gt;</code>, we let the compiler deduce the types for us.</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/bs471n5ce" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>It is possible to make <code>Container</code> a little bit more generic by providing the values as non type template parameters (in case the values you want to use can be used as NTTP):</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;auto...values&gt;
struct Container {

    static constexpr std::tuple t = std::make_tuple (values...);

    decltype(MakeContainerOfS(t)) container = t;

    void Call() {
        std::apply([](auto &amp;...members) { (members.InterfaceMethod(), ...); }, container);
    }
};

int main() {
    Container&lt;1,2.0f&gt;{}.Call();
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/cz5a8bhcn" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
